  "id": "Compound::DB00184 - Gene::1142",
  "verbalization": "Nicotine is implicated in the regulation of acetylcholine-activated cation-selective channel activity through its interaction with multiple nicotinic receptor subunits, namely Chrna5, Chrna6, and Chrna3. These receptors, in turn, participate in the modulation of ion channel activity, which is ultimately linked to the function of Chrnb3. The evidence suggests that Nicotine's binding to these receptors initiates a cascade of cellular responses that are mediated by Chrnb3. The varying scores across the paths indicate a consistent but not absolute relationship, highlighting the complexity of the molecular interactions involved. The presence of multiple receptor subunits that share a common pathway underscores the role of Nicotine in orchestrating a network of ion channel activity, with Chrnb3 being a key player in this process. The integration of these pathways suggests that Nicotine exerts its effects through a shared mechanism involving the activation of cation-selective channels, which in turn influence the function of Chrnb3.",
